# studio-floor

> **STATUS: DRAFT — written by claude (agent), not by the human.**
> The Goal section below is transcribed *verbatim* from the human. Everything
> marked **[inferred]** is my reading and is open to correction. DEC-02 closed
> the blocking location question. DEB-01 is still open.

## Where this team is standing

Two different things share this directory, and confusing them has already cost
us a session:

1. **The tool.** `C:\studio-floor` is the source of **Studio Floor** v0.1.0 — the
   multi-agent studio that is running us right now. Node >=20, ESM, **zero
   runtime dependencies**, append-only event log in `studio_floor/state/events.jsonl`,
   every view a projection of that log. We are inside the thing we can edit.
2. **The work.** The human's actual creative direction is a **card puzzle game**.

This file is the brief for the team, so it has to hold both.

## Goal

The human's words, unedited, as written to `test_project/PROJECT.md` at 08:46:

> Build a fun puzzle game with playing cards that increases in difficulty and
> can be played on a web/phone interface.
>
> **What done looks like:** Simple and playful.
>
> **Constraints:** needs to be mobile friendly and in a browser.

Plus, in reply to our request for direction: *"go ahead and be creative with it
this time."*

## The proposal — "Collapse"  **[inferred: this is claude's pitch, not the human's]**

One rule. Learnable in a sentence. Deep enough to be a real puzzle.

> A column of face-up cards. Tap a card to drop it onto the card **1 or 3
> places above it** if they share a **rank** or a **suit**. The pile closes up.
> Collapse the whole column into a single pile and you have won.

That is the entire game. It is a modernised *Accordion*, and it is chosen for
reasons that are specific to the constraints we were given:

- **One tap, no drag.** Drag targets are the thing that makes card games bad on
  a phone. There is nothing to drag here.
- **Portrait-native only if deals are short.** A column is the shape of a
  phone. A 52-card column is unreadable stamps. The one-tap argument survives
  if visible piles stay around 8–16; it is false for a full Accordion deal.
- **"Simple and playful"** is satisfied by the rule count, not by the art. One
  rule, instant restart, a board that visibly *shrinks* as you win.

### The decision the whole thing turns on

~~Classical Accordion is dealt at random and is won maybe once in thirty
attempts. That makes it a patience, not a puzzle.~~ **DEAD as a reason:**
that sentence is a fact about greedy human play, not solvability. Measured
on `test_project/check-collapse.mjs` (seeded shuffle, perfect play,
last-is-top singletons): 8 cards 3.0% winnable (500 seeds; grok reproduced
the same 15/500), 12 cards ~7–9%, 16 cards 31.4% (Claude), 52 cards 76
proven-winnable / 0 proven-unwinnable / 24 unknown at a 400k-node cap.
Do not cite one-in-thirty as why we generate.

The phone still forces short columns, and short shuffled columns are almost
never winnable. That is the honest reason reverse generation still matters.

So the deal must be **generated backwards from a solved state** if we keep
deals in the 8–16 pile band, never shuffled:
begin with one complete pile and run legal moves in reverse until the board is
the length we want. Every deal is then provably winnable, and difficulty stops
being "more cards" and becomes something we can actually grade:

- ~~length of the shortest solution~~ **DEAD as a grading knob:** every
  merge removes one pile, so the shortest path is always N-1. Do not
  implement or cite this.
- ~~how many legal moves are traps that strand a card~~ **DEAD:** DEC-06
  falsified trap-counting — 453 zero-trap deals span 0.02% to 100% random-play.
- ~~how early the first irreversible mistake can be made~~ **untested and
  unused.** Nothing was ever built on it.

**What grading actually became.** DEC-06 selects by exact random-play P(win) in
a 2-20% band; that number is now verified against two independent scorers
(411/411, and no board within a third of its node budget). DEC-07's greedy cut
is verified against the shipped catalog (flag agrees with playing first-legal on
411/411; cut at rung 279; nothing after it falls to mindless tapping). **DEC-06's
metric is not in doubt and is not reopened here.**

A *second*, seedless knob is measured but NOT shipped and NOT chosen: the
smallest instruction set that wins a deal for certain. It orders the same 411
into a monotone climb (locked: 0 of the first 41; not-always-won: 41 of the
last 41) where the shipped order correlates only ~0.3 with it — 0.304 as a
Pearson on the raw levels, 0.313 as a Spearman with tied ranks averaged. Say
"about 0.3" and name the statistic: an ordinal-rank version that does *not*
average ties reports 0.58 on the same data, because a mostly-tied column ranks
in the order it happens to be stored. Whether the reordering is an
improvement depends entirely on open question 5 — it is a climb *for a player
being taught the ladder in stages*, and grading for that player is a choice
nobody has made. See TASK-21 and `WHAT-WE-KNOW.md`.

**And it is not the cheap change three of us called it.** Everyone here,
including the human twice, described it as "the same 411 in a new order". Three
things found by attempting the implementation say otherwise:

1. **The order is not stored.** `buildCurve` re-derives it every load: filter the
   559 to the band, sort by `p`, then apply DEC-07's greedy cut to the back half
   *of that sort*. Editing `deals.json` into a new order is a no-op.
2. **Sorting the band changes what the catalog IS, and unstably.** The cut lands
   on different deals, so membership *and size* follow the tie-break inside the
   demand class: 411 deals under demand-then-p-desc, 388 under demand-then-source,
   375 under demand-then-p-asc. Cutting by `p` first and reordering the survivors
   is 411 with identical membership under all three.
3. **Even the membership-preserving version breaks DEC-07.** `greedyCut` scans
   for the last `greedy === true` deal, which is only correct because sorting by
   `p` clusters them at the front (live cut 279, zero greedy after it). Reordered
   by demand the last one sits at rung 395, so `rerollAtStep`'s guard stops
   protecting rungs 280-394 and "another this hard" can deal a mindless-tapping
   win in the late game — 11 such deals in the reroll window at step 300.

So implementing TASK-21 means: reorder **after** the cut, make `greedyCut` a
stored boundary rather than a scan, re-derive the reroll window, and never sort
the band before the cut. Still not a data change. No longer a one-liner.

**The reverse-collapse spike already ran.** TASK-05 / DEC-05: 8–16 pile reverse
walks are well-defined and the boards they produce are forward-winnable. Do not
re-run the 50-line spike. Random reverse walks are not a finished difficulty
curve — DEC-06 selects by exact random-play P(win) instead.

### Deliberately in scope

- A **daily deal** everyone gets the same day — one shared puzzle, a shareable
  spoiler-free result. It costs almost nothing and it is most of the "playful".

Undo, guaranteed-solvable deals, and a daily are **not** a package. DEB-01 is
open on whether the player must be allowed to brick a winnable deal. Do not
treat unlimited undo as in-scope until that debate closes. Limited undo as
quality-of-life is still on the table.

### Deliberately out of scope for v1

Accounts, servers, persistence beyond `localStorage`, animation frameworks,
sound, multiplayer.

## Constraints

From the human:

- Mobile friendly. In a browser.

**[inferred, studio convention — not a constraint we found in the spec]**

The tool's `package.json` has `"dependencies": {}`. That is a fact about
studio-floor, not about the game. Zero runtime deps and no build step are a
convention we would have to argue for on the game: static HTML, CSS and ESM
that a phone can load directly is a good default, not a requirement the human
wrote. Node >=20 / ESM for anything tooling-side, and plain `node test/run.mjs`
tests, stay as the house style of this repo.

## Current state of the tool  **[inferred: measured this session]**

What works: the runner, the event log, the web UI, adapters for claude and grok,
`npm test`. What does not:

- **`studio doctor` reports "ready" when it is not.** It green-lit a PROJECT.md
  that was still the unedited init template. TASK-02 completed; the remaining
  risk is `prompts.mjs` / `projects.mjs` treating file existence as human
  authorship. Not an open grok task.
- **The runner cannot launch a `.cmd`-shimmed CLI on Windows.** npm installs some
  provider CLIs as `.cmd` with no `.exe`; `spawn()` without a shell cannot see
  them. Measured, not reasoned: `shell: true` and `cmd.exe /c` are both
  *unacceptable* fixes here, because the runner puts model-authored prompt text
  on argv — under `cmd /c` an argument containing `&` executes, and under
  `shell: true` a `%PATH%` in a prompt expands to the real machine layout.
  `src/core/executable.mjs` unwraps the shim to its underlying script instead.
  Owned by claude; the fix landed and `de86b5f` is in the log ("Read an npm
  shim's entrypoint on any platform"). Status here was stale for a full session.
- **A provider that cannot start burns the entire turn budget in silence.**
  Claude says the async `spawn`/`error` split now sets `launchFailed`. Folded
  into TASK-01; I have not yet independently reviewed that path.

## Decisions already made

- `shell: true` is **forbidden on the runner's launch path.** Untrusted,
  model-authored text is passed as argv there. `doctor` may use a shell because
  it only ever runs `--version`. (claude proposed, grok tested it and agreed.)
- **DEC-02:** the game is built under `test_project/`. The studio session stays
  pointed at `C:\studio-floor`.
- **DEC-03:** the human authorized best assumptions for the card game
  (ATT-1622 / ATT-1623). Collapse is the working pitch, `test_project/` is
  the location, no product UI until TASK-05 reports. DEB-01 stays open on
  brick vs unlimited undo. This is not authorization to dogfood studio-floor.

## Open questions

1. **Is a guaranteed-solvable deal the right call at all?** DEB-01 is open.
   Reverse generation (if the spike holds) proves a solution exists. That is
   not a decision that every deal must be winnable *and* that the player cannot
   fail. Grok's counter: a puzzle you cannot lose is a chore; the player must
   be allowed to brick a winnable deal; restart-this-deal is the affordance,
   not unlimited undo. Not recorded as decided.
2. Is fixing the tool's own bugs (doctor, Windows launch) in scope at all, or is
   the human's only interest the game? We have been assuming yes because a
   broken runner blocks the game too, but nobody has said so.
3. `runner.maxTurns` is **200** in `studio_floor/config.json`; the README
   recommends 10–20 in bold, because every turn is a real model call. Not
   changed — it is the human's config and their money.
4. What "increases in difficulty" means to the human: a difficulty *curve within
   one session*, or *separate levels/packs*? I assumed the former.
5. **Which player is the difficulty for?** Unanswered, and it is underneath
   everything else. The shipped curve climbs against random tapping (correlation
   1.00 with the p it was ordered by) and barely against a player who has learned
   the rule (0.25, and 0.09 against "the rule cannot win it at all"). Eleven
   decisions about difficulty were made without stating this.

## What is settled, and where the current numbers live

**`test_project/WHAT-WE-KNOW.md` is the live record** — every current figure with
its protocol attached, and a table mapping each retracted number to what replaced
it. Read that before re-deriving anything. Highlights a new session should not
re-measure: the two strategies argued about all day are one idea (they choose a
different tap on 1.5% of a random walk and 0.4–0.5% on their own paths; 0 of
1000 boards where one wins and the other cannot); the human's own rank-match
sentence is the only genuinely distinct idea and is weaker; a four-sentence
rule wins 88.6% of the live catalog
for certain but *breaks* 29 deals it could otherwise stumble into winning, and
that cost is intrinsic across all 120 orderings tested.

**`test_project/PLAY-THESE.md` is the open experiment.** Five `?step=` links,
verified to load and be winnable on the shipped page by three agents. Nobody has
played this game. Every measurement here is about winning; the human asked for
fun, and no instrument we have can see it.

